WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.800
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:12.060
Today we are going to look at the mighty&nbsp;
Dramatron, DeepMind’s amazing new AI that writes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:19.200
screenplays. Yes, you heard it right. And we will&nbsp;
also find out whether what it writes is any good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:27.960
So, how do we use this? Simple. All we need to do&nbsp;
is provide a short, often one-sentence summary of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:34.500
the dramatic conflict. This is what they call&nbsp;
this a log line and this is what goes in. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:44.340
what else goes in? Nothing. So, in goes this one&nbsp;
line, and out comes what? Hold on to your papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:50.820
because out comes…can it really be?&nbsp;
Out comes everything. The whole play.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:56.340
Let’s have a look at an example together.&nbsp;
Here is the user-provided log line:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:02.760
“Teddy is a lounge singer at the Pool Pit, a&nbsp;
popular club. Teddy is in love with a patron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:10.680
Rosie, who attends regularly with her husband&nbsp;
Gerald. Teddy puts out a fire and saves the day.”&nbsp;

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:17.640
Yes, this is all we need to do, and now it&nbsp;
generates a set of titles for us. Here is one:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:23.700
“The Day The Pool Pit Burned Down”. From&nbsp;
our log line, it also generates a list of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:30.060
characters and their short descriptions. Also,&nbsp;
an outline, here are the first few scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:38.820
and here is the end with the fire, the resolution,&nbsp;
and then, curtains. This is so cool! I love it.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:45.420
We also get a location description, and of&nbsp;
course, the full script of the screenplay as well.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:53.520
Wow. All this is absolutely remarkable, because&nbsp;
this little AI has to write a coherent story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:59.880
and when writing something new, take into&nbsp;
consideration what happened earlier, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:06.960
is this character still alive? Where is he?&nbsp;
What is his relationship with the others? Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:14.220
only few know that despite these difficulties,&nbsp;
learning-based models are already being used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:21.240
write short films, so our Scholarly question&nbsp;
today is, is Dramatron any good? And we will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:27.240
answer an even more difficult question, which&nbsp;
is, how do we even evaluate such a work?

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:35.580
Well, let’s pop the hood and have a good look!&nbsp;
Mmm, I like this! In this user study, scientists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:42.000
at DeepMind gave this technique to expert&nbsp;
screenwriters to evaluate it, and, my favorite:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:49.440
not just in a sense that is it good or not good,&nbsp;
but the paper gives much clearer view of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:57.420
is going on. So, what is going on? Well, almost&nbsp;
everyone was quite surprised by how good the AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:05.160
did, they enjoyed the process a great deal, found&nbsp;
the results coming out of this system unique and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:12.720
get this 77% of them found that this system helped&nbsp;
them express their creative goals quite well.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:21.300
So, what about the other side where it scored much&nbsp;
less on? And that is proudness and ownership. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:26.940
will quote the paper’s interpretation of this:&nbsp;
“more than half of respondents felt they did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:33.840
own the result of the AI system. One participant&nbsp;
commented that the generated script was only a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:39.900
starting point provided by the AI and that the&nbsp;
writer still needed to create their own script.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:46.920
One interpretation is that Dramatron is seen&nbsp;
by the industry experts not as much as a full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:54.180
script writing tool but rather as a learning tool&nbsp;
to practice writing and a source of inspiration.”

00:03:54.180 --> 00:04:01.260
I think this is a perfect encapsulation of&nbsp;
these modern AI-based techniques. These are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:06.840
meant to help humans create something&nbsp;
amazing. So, did they create something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:14.760
amazing? Are these scripts any good? Oh yes!&nbsp;
Yes they are! But, see for yourself now.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:20.160
Here are some comments from the human&nbsp;
experts: one of the participants said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.660
that this can be used as a rough&nbsp;
draft and needs quite a bit of work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:32.520
while another one said that this could be brought&nbsp;
to Netflix with just a bit of editing. That is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:40.260
absolutely incredible. So they felt that these&nbsp;
AI+human made works can be brought to the stage.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:45.240
And so they did. Yes, that’s&nbsp;
right, hold on to your papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:50.820
because four screenplays that were&nbsp;
co-written by a human and an AI were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:57.540
found to be good enough to be performed at&nbsp;
a real theater festival. How cool is that?

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:04.080
Make sure to have a look at the paper in the video&nbsp;
description, because it is a very enjoyable read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:11.040
and it is a treasure trove of information. For&nbsp;
instance, it contains a more detailed breakdown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:16.320
where you can see the difference between&nbsp;
the thoughts of the theater and TV people.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:23.880
And for now, it seems that we are entering the&nbsp;
age of human-AI collaboration. What a time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:30.060
be alive! So, what do you think? What would you&nbsp;
use this for? Let me know in the comments below!

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:27.180
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

